From: jfy9606 <jfy9606@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 18:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] SDBM_File: guard the malloc/free declarations with MYMALLOC

Perl 5.28's bundled SDBM_File re-declares the libc allocator using perl's
legacy typedefs:

    extern Malloc_t malloc(MEM_SIZE);
    extern Free_t   free(Malloc_t);

Those typedefs come from the generated config.h.  When Configure picks the
system allocator (malloc_type="MALLOC", i.e. MYMALLOC undefined) they stay
Malloc_t=char * and Free_t=int, so the declarations conflict with the
<stdlib.h> prototypes (void *malloc(size_t) / void free(void *)) that
sdbm.h has just included.  GCC 14+/glibc 2.4x treat an incompatible
redeclaration of a builtin as a hard error, so building the host perl fails:

    sdbm.c:38:17: error: conflicting types for 'malloc'; have 'char *(size_t)'
    sdbm.c:39:15: error: conflicting types for 'free'; have 'int(char *)'

Only emit those declarations when perl actually uses its own malloc: in that
case sdbm.h maps malloc/free to Perl_malloc/Perl_mfree (whose prototypes match
Malloc_t/Free_t) and nothing clashes with <stdlib.h>.  Otherwise the standard
declarations already in scope are used as-is.

Tested with gcc 15.2.0 / glibc 2.43 by rebuilding ext/SDBM_File with the
original compile line.

Signed-off-by: jfy9606 <jfy9606@gmail.com>
---
 ext/SDBM_File/sdbm.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/ext/SDBM_File/sdbm.c
+++ b/ext/SDBM_File/sdbm.c
@@ -35,8 +35,13 @@
 extern "C" {
 #endif
 
+/* Only declare the allocator entry points when perl uses its own malloc: otherwise
+ * these conflict with the <stdlib.h> prototypes above (Malloc_t is "char *" and Free_t
+ * is "int" in that configuration), which modern gcc/glibc reject as hard errors. */
+#ifdef MYMALLOC
 extern Malloc_t malloc(MEM_SIZE);
 extern Free_t free(Malloc_t);
+#endif
 
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
